1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:21,501
By morning, the city had put my face on a bus. Not an advertisement.
The whole front of the bus was my face. The driver looked out through
my sunglasses, and when it turned a corner my left cheek opened to let
people off. Tank watched it pass beneath our balcony. Nigga, they made
your head public transportation.

2
00:00:21,751 --> 00:00:41,426
About time the people rode in something great. Rosa was eating
breakfast in his lap despite there being twelve empty chairs. She
leaned forward to see the bus, then looked back at me. You charging
for that? Porsche answered from the next chair. Now we are. It was the
first official race day.

3
00:00:41,676 --> 00:01:04,240
The Grand Prix of Kings lasted a month, with seven major races,
exhibitions, sea events and enough parties to make a man forget why he
had brought his passport. The ceremonial qualifier had put me first on
the grid. Today's Crown Circuit would take us through the city three
times, then climb to the glass palace above the harbor.

4
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:28,266
Winner received the first of seven jeweled keys. All seven together
opened the prize ceremony. The points decided who held them at the
end. I liked a competition that gave a nigga jewelry for doing his
job. Lark had spent the night in the garage with my car. When I went
down, she stood beside it holding a mug that read WORLD'S WORST
PASSENGER.

5
00:01:28,516 --> 00:01:47,845
Your wife bought me this, she said. She knows talent. Shawna came down
behind me carrying a little bag of breakfast. She put it on Lark's
workbench. Eat. I will. Now. Lark took a piece of bread out without
arguing. I respected how quickly Shawna had become the chief of all
this.

6
00:01:48,095 --> 00:02:11,740
Cassius waited near the far wall in a fresh racing jacket, arm still
in the sling. His replacement car sat under a black cover. You racing?
I asked. No. Doctor said another impact could tear the shoulder worse.
Nigga, listen to the doctor. I am. I hate it. He walked with me around
the Lamborghini. Watch the third descent.

7
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:32,675
There's a blind rise before the old customs house. Yesterday's
practice showed a dip after it. Anything else? He looked toward Lark.
She nodded. The green harbor cars have disappeared. Every one of them.
The service company says they changed the fleet overnight. Fast
workers. Frightened workers.

8
00:02:32,925 --> 00:02:56,923
Luc had slept in one of our extra rooms, with Tank's jacket over a
chair outside the door as a warning anybody with survival instincts
could understand. He had told us the staff were being moved to Cinder
Dock on Friday, four nights away. From there, boats took them to the
island's old shipyard. Nobody who went there came back to work the
mainland hotels.

9
00:02:57,173 --> 00:03:19,762
He didn't know what happened to them. That was bad enough without
inventing worse. Chief Knox called while I was putting on my gloves.
Captain, she said, why is there footage of your patrol vehicle on a
boat? International waters. It was on two boats. International boats.
There was a pause. Did you save that driver? Yes, ma'am. Good.

10
00:03:20,012 --> 00:03:41,128
The department has received eleven marriage proposals, three requests
for transfer and a yacht club membership. Keep the membership. Already
did. I smiled. Then I told her about Luc, the missing staff and the
duke's fleet. She went quiet in a different way. Keep your people
close, she said. Your badge gets attention.

11
00:03:41,378 --> 00:04:07,487
Attention can open a door or tell the wrong man which door you're
standing behind. I know. And Aiden? Yes, ma'am? Win the race. Nigga,
that was leadership. The grid sat beneath an arch covered in blue
flowers. The street beyond it climbed between grand hotels, then fell
toward the port. Thousands of people pressed behind the barriers. More
watched from balconies, windows, rooftops.

12
00:04:07,737 --> 00:04:26,851
A choir had been hired to sing the race anthem, but half of them
started chanting my name when I got out of the car. The conductor
tried to recover the key. Lost it completely. Shawna kissed me beside
the driver's door. She wore a blue team jacket with AIDEN on the back
in gold.

13
00:04:27,101 --> 00:04:52,051
Under my name she had added HUSBAND, because apparently the women of
Monaco required a second line of clarification. Inez walked over in
her red racing suit. You running the police car all month? she asked.
Why? Because I want to know whether being arrested is going to count
as losing. Depends how fast you confess. She grinned. Then her
expression turned serious.

14
00:04:52,301 --> 00:05:13,946
My father read the rest of Amalia's letter last night. He wants to
show you after the race. What'd he find? A reason to hate himself.
Tell him wait till we know where to put it. She looked toward the
duke's balcony. Aurelio stood beside Bellario under an awning, both
dressed in white, like innocence was a team color.

15
00:05:14,196 --> 00:05:39,051
I told my father I'm winning today, Inez said. Nigga, you can tell him
you drove beautifully. She punched my shoulder lightly. Try to keep
your car out of the architecture. The anthem finally ended. Drivers
climbed in. The crowd grew tight and loud around us. A marshal leaned
through the window to ask whether my police lights were part of the
car's original equipment.

16
00:05:39,301 --> 00:06:02,602
"Nigga, they're part of the car's personality." "They may distract
other drivers." "My face does that. You want me to race backward?"
Shawna bent at the waist laughing. The marshal tried to stay serious
and failed around the mouth. "Please use them only when necessary."
"Nigga, I am a necessary situation." Tank pointed at the marshal from
behind the barrier.

17
00:06:02,852 --> 00:06:22,332
"That nigga about to learn." "All of them," Porsche said. I put both
hands on the wheel. For a moment I thought of Vegas. Big T's lot. My
first night. The heat of the gun, the hurt in my side, Shawna looking
at me like she couldn't decide whether I was rescue or a different
kind of disaster.

18
00:06:22,582 --> 00:06:40,247
Now she stood behind a race barrier across an ocean wearing my name,
and a city was waiting for me to do something impossible before lunch.
Nigga, life had finally started acting like it knew who I was. The
lights went out. Inez launched beside me.

19
00:06:40,497 --> 00:06:58,584
A yellow car took the outside, its driver a narrow man called Prince
Bastian who had introduced himself three times at dinner and seemed
offended that no one had introduced him a fourth. He swept ahead into
the first turn. I let him enjoy it until the second.

20
00:06:58,834 --> 00:07:22,002
Then I passed him so close he looked over like I had entered his
living room without knocking. The city became motion. White stone,
blue water, red banners. We passed the opera steps with Inez half a
car behind. She came alongside on the descent, and for two turns we
ran together, close enough that I could see her eyes through the
visor.

21
00:07:22,252 --> 00:07:45,735
She was smiling. I smiled back. The nigga in the yellow car tried to
fit between us. There was no between us. He backed off at the last
second and nearly lost his rear wheels to the curb. Inez shook her
head inside her helmet. I laughed once, sharp and happy, and we
dropped into the tunnel beneath the old city. Inside, the engines
multiplied.

22
00:07:45,985 --> 00:08:05,759
Mine roared off the walls. Hers answered. Lights ran across the hood
in long white bars. At the exit she took the lead by a nose. I could
have called the blue thunder. I didn't. That mattered to me. I liked
knowing the difference between what I could do and what the power
could add.

23
00:08:06,009 --> 00:08:24,140
The thunder was a burst, a loan against the strength in my body. I
wasn't spending it because a beautiful woman had better timing through
one tunnel. I caught her at the harbor bend. The first lap ended with
us side by side. Above the crowd I heard Tank's voice.

24
00:08:24,390 --> 00:08:46,014
I couldn't make out the words, but the volume suggested he had
physically challenged the entire spectator area to doubt me. On the
second lap, things got ugly. A white car came out of the service lane
ahead of us. It belonged to the race, had a marshal's stripe on the
side and should never have been moving across the active road.

25
00:08:46,264 --> 00:09:10,735
It stopped sideways at the entrance to the third descent. Inez swore
over the open race channel. Obstacle! She turned sharply, barely
missing it. Her red car hit a loose section of barrier and spun toward
a staircase descending into the market below. I pulled left. The
yellow car behind me hit the stopped marshal's car. Metal folded.
Smoke burst across the road.

26
00:09:10,985 --> 00:09:35,765
Somebody's horn started and didn't stop. Inez's car slid backward down
the first three steps. People in the market scattered. Nigga, no. I
took my car onto the staircase beside her. The first impact drove the
breath out of me. The second made my teeth meet. I kept the
Lamborghini straight while her car bounced against the stone rail,
scraping sparks from the wall.

27
00:09:36,015 --> 00:09:59,852
We descended side by side, two beautiful machines doing something no
manufacturer had put in the brochure. At the bottom stood a flower
stall, an old stone well and a crowd trapped between them. I hit the
horn. People moved. Inez's car wasn't going to stop in time. I drove
across her path and caught her front corner against the rear of mine.

28
00:10:00,102 --> 00:10:22,204
The impact spun us both. My shoulder hit the door. Flowers exploded
across the windshield. We came to rest around the stone well, her car
on one side, mine on the other, with a wreath hanging from my mirror
like I had won a funeral. For one second all I heard was ticking
metal. Inez! I'm here. You hurt? My pride.

29
00:10:22,454 --> 00:10:45,998
Nigga, it needed room anyway. She laughed once, then groaned. I was
out before she opened her door. Her ankle had twisted beneath the
pedals. She could move it, but not without pain. I helped her onto the
low stone wall around the well. A woman from the flower stall came
over holding two huge bouquets. For you, she said breathlessly.

30
00:10:46,248 --> 00:11:10,045
Ma'am, I just destroyed your business. You saved my sister. Behind
her, another woman was crying and pointing at the place where Inez's
car would have gone. The flower seller pushed the bouquets into my
hands. Take them. Nigga, what was I supposed to do? Refuse flowers
while standing in a car crash shaped like a love letter? I handed one
bouquet to Inez.

31
00:11:10,295 --> 00:11:34,232
First date on the mountain, second in a well. You moving fast. Tell
your wife I want a refund. The crowd laughed. It helped. Fear loosened
enough for people to start moving again. My radio crackled. Shawna's
voice came through, low and urgent. Talk to me. I'm good. Inez twisted
her ankle. We're both out. Your chest? Fine. Your side? Fine, baby.

32
00:11:34,482 --> 00:11:56,502
She took one breath I could hear all the way from the grid. Then get
her help. The marshals reached us two minutes later. One tried to tell
me to stand aside. I showed him my badge, pointed to Inez and told him
to be useful in that direction. Above us, at the top of the staircase,
Bellario appeared. He was speaking into a radio.

33
00:11:56,752 --> 00:12:17,964
Smiling. Not at me. At somebody I couldn't see. I looked down at the
white marshal's stripe reflected in the broken glass on my hood. Then
up at his radio. Something in me went still. Inez followed my eyes.
You see it too, she said. I'm starting. The race continued around us.
The leaders had gone.

34
00:12:18,214 --> 00:12:43,749
The announcer's voice drifted over the buildings, saying Captain Aiden
had made another courageous sacrifice and would surely receive the
admiration of the entire principality. Admiration was nice. Nigga, I
wanted my key. Lark arrived with Tank and a service crew. She went
straight to the Lamborghini, checked the wheels, looked beneath the
front and stood back with both hands on her hips.

35
00:12:43,999 --> 00:13:01,902
You are a violent owner. She still run? She is offended. She still
runs. I got in. Tank leaned through the window. Aiden. What? You
serious? I looked at him. He stepped back and slapped the roof. Go get
them niggas. There was no easy road out of the market.

36
00:13:02,152 --> 00:13:23,763
There was, however, an arcade through the old customs building, a
broad set of doors and a courtyard that opened onto the last climb.
Lark looked at the route. Looked at me. Then she moved her tools out
of the way. I drove through the customs house with both bouquets on
the passenger seat. A guard dropped his sandwich.

37
00:13:24,013 --> 00:13:47,003
I shouted an apology through the window because I respected lunch. We
came out on the climb half a lap behind the leaders. My car's rear
panel flapped. The steering pulled a little. There were petals in
every vent. Nigga, we looked like the world's most expensive prom had
escaped from a hurricane. I drove anyway. One car at the old palace.

38
00:13:47,253 --> 00:14:11,574
Another beside the railway. Two through the harbor tunnel. The crowd
saw the broken blue Lamborghini and rose in sections, pointing,
shouting, spreading my return faster than the announcer could
understand it. By the final lap I was fourth. By the glass palace I
was third. Ahead, Prince Bastian's yellow car chased a silver coupe
toward the line.

39
00:14:11,824 --> 00:14:35,322
Behind them rolled the white pace car that should have withdrawn at
the final corner. Instead, it slowed across the road. Bellario stood
at the finish with the jeweled key on a cushion. Nigga wanted me
stopped one more time. I put on my siren. The crowd screamed. The pace
car didn't move. I leaned out the window and pointed straight at its
driver.

40
00:14:35,572 --> 00:14:58,969
You are under arrest, motherfucker! He looked back. That was his
mistake. The silver coupe went left. Bastian went right. I went
straight, not into the pace car but across the low marble divider
beside it, the Lamborghini lifting just enough to make every camera on
the finish line tilt upward. For a breath I was above all three of
them.

41
00:14:59,219 --> 00:15:22,810
Then I landed beyond the pace car, the steering wheel kicking against
my hands, and crossed the line first with my siren singing louder than
the anthem. The palace windows shook with the crowd. I stopped,
climbed out and walked back toward the pace car. Its driver had both
hands up already. Bellario came toward me with his radio against his
chest.

42
00:15:23,060 --> 00:15:45,356
Captain, the result requires review. Nigga, review this. I pointed to
the driver. Who told him to stay on the track? A communication error.
Who told the other car to block the descent? An unfortunate sequence—
Keep talking. You might confess by accident. The cameras moved closer.
Bellario saw them. Saw the crowd.

43
00:15:45,606 --> 00:16:05,244
Saw the duke on the glass balcony, watching him with a face that
offered no rescue. He lifted the cushion. The first key, he announced,
voice suddenly enormous, to Captain Aiden. The driver of the pace car
pointed at Bellario. "That man told me—" "Nigga, I know which man," I
said.

44
00:16:05,494 --> 00:16:30,835
"I'm asking why you let that nigga choose where your car stopped."
"He's the marshal." "And I'm the nigga you tried to stop. See how the
second title got important?" Tank arrived beside me. "Nigga, keep
pointing. We love directions." Bellario lifted the cushion higher.
"The key, Captain." "Look at this nigga changing the subject with
jewelry," Porsche said. "Good jewelry?" Mercedes asked.

45
00:16:31,085 --> 00:16:50,861
"We'll ask Marcellus. Nigga ain't bribing us with plate twice." I took
the key. Then I took his radio. Evidence, I said. He looked like he
wanted to bite me. Shawna came through the crowd at a run. I caught
her with my good arm, lifted her off the ground and kissed her while
the city shouted around us.

46
00:16:51,111 --> 00:17:14,789
You brought me flowers, she said when she saw the car. Whole market.
You're bleeding. Small amount. Nigga. I loved when she said it like
that. Like my entire government name had disappointed her in one
syllable. She pressed a cloth to the scrape above my eyebrow. Behind
her, Inez arrived in the medical cart with her ankle wrapped and both
bouquets in her lap.

47
00:17:15,039 --> 00:17:37,347
She had kept mine safe. You won in that? she asked, looking at my car.
Car wanted it bad. She shook her head slowly. I've raced the best
drivers in the world. Now you have. She laughed, then raised her
bouquet toward the crowd. Aiden! she shouted. The crowd answered so
loudly the duke stepped back from his balcony.

48
00:17:37,597 --> 00:18:01,129
That night, Lark laid Bellario's confiscated radio on our dining
table. It had gone silent the moment I took it. Nothing useful
remained on it, and we weren't going to invent a miracle because a
radio looked important. But Luc recognized the little silver mark
etched on the back. A white bird. The Sovereign Sun uses that mark, he
said.

49
00:18:01,379 --> 00:18:17,574
The old crew put it on their equipment. Cassius reached into his
pocket and put his father's little metal bird beside it. Same wings.
Same lowered head. Inez unfolded Amalia's letter. Now, she said, we
read the whole thing.
